{#
  _network_axis.j2 — the SINGLE renderer for the per-service network axis.

  Every compose template that honors a service's `network:` key imports these
  macros rather than writing the conditionals itself. That is the whole point of
  the file: the axis is rendered in one place, so a set of templates cannot
  drift into a set of dialects that each spell "run this on the host network"
  slightly differently.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  IMPORTING
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The macros take everything they need as parameters, so a plain (context-free)
  import is correct — never `with context`:

    Service templates, rendered by an Environment rooted at the PROJECT root
    (deployment/compose_generator.py's `FileSystemLoader(".")`, template paths
    like `services/<name>/docker-compose.yml.j2`):

      {% import "services/_network_axis.j2" as net %}

    Templates rendered by an Environment rooted at this directory import by
    bare name instead:

      {% import "_network_axis.j2" as net %}

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE `svc` ARGUMENT
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Every macro takes the service's own config block as its first argument — the
  `services.<name>` mapping of the rendered config.yml:

      {{- net.network(services.event_dispatcher) }}

  It may arrive as a mapping (the usual case), as None, or undefined; the last
  two mean "the service declares nothing", which renders the default behaviour.
  The one key read here is `network`:

    unset / `bridge`  the service joins the project's compose network
    `host`            the service shares the host's network namespace

  Values are validated where the profile is parsed, so an unrecognized value
  never reaches a render; a macro that somehow sees one renders the default
  (network-joined) shape rather than inventing a topology.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHITESPACE CONTRACT
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  A macro emits a LEADING newline and no trailing one, and is called alone on
  its own line with a left-trim marker:

      {{- net.network(services.event_dispatcher) }}

  That pairing is what makes one idiom cover both uses. Replacing a block a
  template already carries reproduces it exactly — the `{{-` eats the newline
  ending the previous line, the macro supplies it back. Adding a block that is
  usually absent costs nothing when the axis is unset — the macro emits the
  empty string and the whole line disappears, rather than leaving the blank line
  an `{% if %}`/`{% endif %}` pair would have left.

  Mind the `{{-`: it strips ALL preceding whitespace, so do not place a call
  where a deliberate blank line precedes it.
#}

{#- Service-level network attachment.

    The default joins `network_name`, the compose network the project creates.
    `host` shares the host's network namespace instead, which is what a service
    needing broadcast traffic (control-system discovery) or the host's own
    published ports requires. Pair with `network_declaration()` for the
    file-level stanza, and with `ports()` — which suppresses published ports
    under `host`, where compose has no port map to publish. -#}
{%- macro network(svc, indent=4, network_name='osprey-network') -%}
{%- set s = svc if svc else {} -%}
{%- set mode = s.network | default('bridge', true) -%}
{%- set pad = ' ' * indent -%}
{%- if mode == 'host' -%}
{{ ['', pad ~ 'network_mode: host'] | join('\n') }}
{%- else -%}
{{ ['', pad ~ 'networks:', ' ' * (indent + 2) ~ '- ' ~ network_name] | join('\n') }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#- File-level `networks:` stanza matching `network()`.

    Under `host` nothing is declared: no service in the file joins a network, so
    a declaration would create one nobody attaches to. -#}
{%- macro network_declaration(svc, indent=0, network_name='osprey-network') -%}
{%- set s = svc if svc else {} -%}
{%- set mode = s.network | default('bridge', true) -%}
{%- set pad = ' ' * indent -%}
{%- if mode == 'host' -%}
{%- else -%}
{{ ['', pad ~ 'networks:', ' ' * (indent + 2) ~ network_name ~ ':'] | join('\n') }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#- Published ports, suppressed entirely under `host`.

    `entries` is a list of compose port mappings as bare strings
    (`127.0.0.1:10010:10010`); each is emitted double-quoted, since an unquoted
    `hh:mm`-shaped mapping is a YAML sexagesimal trap. Under `host` there is no
    port map to publish — compose rejects `ports:` alongside
    `network_mode: host` on some runtimes and ignores it on others — so nothing
    is emitted and the service's ports are simply the host's. -#}
{%- macro ports(svc, entries=[], indent=4) -%}
{%- set s = svc if svc else {} -%}
{%- set mode = s.network | default('bridge', true) -%}
{%- set published = entries | default([], true) -%}
{%- if mode != 'host' and published -%}
{%- set ns = namespace(lines=['', ' ' * indent ~ 'ports:']) -%}
{%- for entry in published -%}
{%- set _ = ns.lines.append(' ' * (indent + 2) ~ '- "' ~ entry ~ '"') -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{{ ns.lines | join('\n') }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}
